/* book exam traps: A single gated D latch is level-sensitive, not edge-triggered. Only the master-slave pair, or another edge circuit, is a register. | Setup uses the longest path and contains T; hold uses the shortest path and has no T. Slowing the clock fixes setup, not hold. | A dynamic latch has a minimum clock frequency; a static latch works down to DC. A dynamic transmission-gate register also fails on clock overlap, which C2MOS and TSPC are designed to survive. | In a shift register the first bit sent ends up in the last stage. */
